 ],
 "notes": "27 stations: Nos. 4-29 plus 7B, all in one column with no wrapped rows. Fragments are the full printed lines, leading spaces included, because the asterisks' horizontal positions carry meaning.\n\nMarks: Each asterisk sits under one of the column heads \"1879, '80, '81.\" (line 182). Its position shows the year of the return: character 4 = 1879, 9 = '80, 14 = '81. In the marks field I give the asterisks as printed, with the year(s) I read from their positions in brackets. The asterisks in the '81 position total 12 (Nos. 5, 7, 8, 10, 14, 15, 16, 19, 24, 26, 27, 28), which matches the 12 the other reader counted. Fair Isle (7B) has no asterisks, as you'd expect for a station added that year.\n\nNumbers: Printed with a trailing full stop (\"4.\", \"7B.\"). I dropped the stop. The list prints \"7B\"; the text elsewhere calls it \"No. 7 b.\"\n\nHeights (other): Only the first row has \"ft.\" (\"230 ft.\"). Every row below has a ditto mark (\"145 \\\"\") in place of \"ft.\", and I copied those as printed. Fair Isle's height is printed \"?  \\\"\" (unknown).\n\nKeepers: I dropped the full stop that ends each line. Fair Isle's keeper is printed \"--Lawrence.\"; the dash presumably stands for a forename the compiler didn't have, and I kept it. Stations with no keeper printed have keepers [].\n\nCoast: The first five rows (Shetland stations and Fair Isle) sit straight under the table heading \"East Coast of Scotland.\" with no sub-head of their own, so I used that heading. The other rows use the printed sub-heads \"Orkney\" and \"Mainland\". No type (L.H./L.V.) and no light characters are printed in this list.\n\nPossible errors, kept as printed:\n- No. 4 \"North Uist, Shetland\": North Uist is in the Outer Hebrides, not Shetland. This is almost certainly an error for North Unst (Muckle Flugga), from either the print or the transcription.\n- \"Whalsey\" is the period spelling of Whalsay. \"Neven Kerr\" may be a misprint of Nevin/Niven.\n- \"Chanonry Point, Elgin\": Chanonry is in Ross-shire, not Elgin. The \"Elgin\" may have been carried down from Covesea on the next line. This fits the earlier finding of a Chanonry slip in the 1879 print.\n- \"Montroseness, Aberdeen\": Scurdie Ness/Montrose is in Forfarshire, so \"Aberdeen\" looks like the same kind of carry-over.\n- \"Cromarty, East Cromarty\" and \"Tarbat Ness, East Ross\" are given as printed.\n- \"Inch Keith\" is printed as two words, \"Girdleness\" as one, and \"Montroseness\" as one.",
